            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_Ret, "If the return value is true, index of x in new array is >= 0");

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that Array list count remains same (or becomes bigger)
            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount, "Array list count remains same (or becomes bigger)");

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that index of x in new array is same as old array or is bigger
            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX && New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "index of x in new array is same as old array or is bigger");

        }
    }
}

I'm using Pex with Gallio and NUnit to create a test. I'm stuck with the following test:
When I run this test with Assumes, the test does not run at all, presumably because it's failing the assumption. 
Can anyone provide guidance on why the test would fail to run given the assumptions?
Thanks in advance.

A: The issue here is that Pex only generates tests which pass all assumptions. So if your assumption fails for any input, Pex will not generate a test case.
In your case, there seems to be a logical issue with your assumption. If "arrList.Count > 0" is true, then "arrList.Contains(x)" should not be able to fail. This is because "arrList.Contains(x)" checks if arrList contains x and will return false if arrList is empty.
If you can ensure that your "arrList.Count > 0" is always true, then your assumption should pass and the test should run.
Make sure your assumptions are correct and cover all the edge cases in your actual test. 

A: When using Pex with [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute, the assumption should be correct according to the specification of your method (e.g., the preconditions of the method). In other words, the assumption should cover the test cases where the method should work correctly. 
In your test case, it seems you have assumed that the list will always contain at least one element and are trying to use the contains method on an empty list.
In your method, the precondition has been stated to ensure the arraylist should not be empty. So, the assumption should be that the list should not be empty. 
So change your assumption as follows:
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Count > 0);
    ...
}

This will make sure that your assumption ensures your method under test should work correctly and Pex will consider all your test cases accordingly.
Note: If this doesn't help then more code will be required to understand the reason behind the error.

A: 
When I run this test with Assumes, the test does not run at all, presumably because it's failing the assumption.

Actually, your understanding is wrong. In your code [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute indicates that this method should be executed with a specified set of input parameters, it doesn't mean that the test cases that don’t satisfy the assumption will not be generated. It just means that the test is executed under a specific set of inputs. 
As a result, if one test case violates the assumption, there will be a warning message show up in your test result. But this doesn't stop the test run, as long as no test case violates the assumption, all test cases should be executed. 
In your case, the assumption is wrong because the arraylist under test can't find the element x, causing the test case not passing the assumption. But the actual test cases under other combinations of inputs are still running. This is a desirable behaviour. 
You could add additional code to handle the case where x is not found in arrList (i.e., its index would be -1), or arrange your tests to never have x in arrList and run them again to make sure your assumptions are correct. 
In addition,  you need to add more postconditions or validates in your tests to ensure the output has been tested according to the input values and the actual behaviors of method.
For all code testing, always keep the initial assumption as far as possible, it’s quite important as it helps optimize the test automation.
And this problem also can be solved by changing your Pex test generation settings, by config--------------------	    int tempIndex = 0;
	    if (New_Ret)
	    {
		    tempIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x);
	    }
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret && tempIndex >= 0);

            // we expect the list and index information to be the same if Contains returns 'true'
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0)
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            if (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0)
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0));
        }
    }
}

