The "arrList.IndexOf(x)" part checks the index of the value "x" in the arraylist. Similarly, "arrList.LastIndexOf(x)" checks for the last index of the value "x" present in the list.
The expected behavior of the method under test is that it should return true if arraylist contains x and false otherwise. With the observed values we can check whether the method worked correctly or not.
The "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" part is used to specify additional conditions which must be true about the observable properties.

A: If you want to validate the behavior of the ArrayList's indexOf method, you need to ensure the conditions in your `Debug.Assert` statements are met, namely:

1. **If the return value is `true`, index of x in new array is either equal to `Old_arrListIndexOfX` or `Old_arrListLastIndexOfX`  because indexOf method can return the position of the first occurrence of an item in the ArrayList.**

2. **If the return value is `false`, index of x in new array is less than 0. i.e., the value `x` is not found in the ArrayList.**

But please note, `LastIndexOf(x)` won't always return the same index value as `IndexOf(x)`, especially if the ArrayList contains duplicate values of `x`. So a value of `false` for return from `Contains(x)` might mean that `LastIndexOf(x)` found index value less than `IndexOf(x)`.

Make sure to have a look at the ArrayList indexOf and Count property behaviors, these conditions can vary. Check ArrayList's documentation for further details.

P.S: Pex might not be properly handling the scenarios where the ArrayList has multiple occurrences of the same value. It will pick elements at index(es) based on its order, not on the count of the occurrence of an element. Hence, your assertions may not hold true.

This is a bit off topic but may also be of interest, one of the best practices is to make sure your code is resilient and not dependent on specific values as Pex might generate it. It does its best for exploring different scenarios, but it's not guaranteed to cover all possible cases.

Overall, this kind of tests might be very useful to make sure your code behaves predictably and to some extent detect for edge cases. But it should still be used as a tool in your process of verification and not as the sole verification tool. Make sure to validate these tests against your functional requirements.

And as a side note, since this is a legacy code base and you don't have any other means of verifying its correctness, Pex can provide good coverage of edge cases that have been found in further testing of its results.

You need to make sure that the tests that are being built are aligning with the verification requirements of the project. If tests are not aligned properly, they might become a nuisance for testing purposes, adding overhead to the verification process, whereas they may provide valuable results for ensuring the overall performance of the application.

I hope this should give you an understanding of the situation and hopefully, it helps you with your tests.


A: When using PEX with the ArrayList, it is important to consider some potential edge cases:


*

*If you create an ArrayList and then Add an item, the Count is increased by 1. Similarly, if you Remove an item, the Count is decreased. But some methods do not change the Count and thus should not change according to a PEx test. For example, 'Contains' method merely iterates through the list looking for the item and should not affect the Count.

*Similarly, the indexOf method could return 0 (the first index of an element) but the LastIndexOf method could return something else if the list contains duplicates.

*Also note, in a PEX testing, an item could be added at an index that is not the current count or could also be added at the end of the list--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0));
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is true, last index of x in new array is >= index of x in new array
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is false, count in new array must not changed
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount));
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is false, index of x in new array must not changed
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX));
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is false, last index of x in new array must not changed
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX));

            PexAssume.Value(New_Ret);
        }
    }
}

However, using this test case, the array list count, indexOf(X), and lastIndexOf(X) won't change, and they remain equal to the precondition. I think there's a problem with the PexAssume.Value(New_Ret) in the test case. 
When I tried to test this case, the test passed, which is unexpected since we had assumed New_Ret is false and it also returns false in our post-condition.
Please assist me to understand the problem and help me fix it.

A: Unfortunately, Partial Order Contracts in Pex/Differential Testing are subject to limitation and there are known issues when dealing with custom classes that utilize indexers (such as ArrayList in your case). So your current approach isn't likely to work as expected.
Pex generates partial order contracts for each method call in the static context. It assumes that an method call with the same parameters gives the same result. When dealing with indexers(e.g. ArrayList), it doesn't capture that the indexer actually gives us an IEnumerable, and changes in one operation may not be reflected in the contract. 
So we are not able to properly differentiate between PUT and GET operation for indexer with ArrayList due to current limitations. 
The best practice in your scenario would be to separate your test cases for PUT and GET. 
I would recommend considering using Moles for tests on actual methods when dealing with custom classes. 
Here is more information about the limitations and some suggestions how to ignore certain methods: 
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/moles/default.aspx
It's not perfect, but it could help.
I strongly recommend going through Microsoft Research Moles Tutorials on MSDN before attempting to use it:
http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/hh531444.aspx
Also, check out the forums for any recent updates/fixes:
http://social.msdn.microsoft.com/Forums/en-US/home?forum=cdemilk
You may want to consider writing your own tests for the indexer.
Hope this helps and best of luck with your project.

A: The test case is running fine and passes as expected. The issues I was facing was with the static pre-conditions and post-conditions. It seems that PEx does not allow different post-conditions for the PUT method and the GET method for the value method property in an ArrayList. That's why it's only running the PUT method with one pre- and post-condition. If there are any known limitations in PEx regarding such scenarios, please let me know so I can update this accordingly.
Regards,
Nandisha

A: For situations where you want to use differential testing (Partial Order Contracts) with custom classes like ArrayList, there are essentially two ways to go.


*

*Separate the List Operations into two different test methods. As suggested by Andrey Astrakhantsev, creating a separate test method for the Add and Remove operations could work.

*Use a Different Framework for your testing - such as QuickCheck or NUnit.


For example:
Here are the NUnit tests for ArrayList--------------------            int xIndex = -1;
            if (New_Ret)
            {
                xIndex = New_arrListIndexOfX;
            }
            int expected = xIndex >= 0;
            PexAssume.Equals(expected, 1);
            
            // Assert is here -> Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == (New_arrListIndexOfX > -1 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX > -1));

            Assert.AreEqual(false, Old_arrListCount > New_arrListCount, "The count of the array should not have changed.");
        }
    }
}
```
This test, as before, ensures that an object containing the value x still contains x. It observes some important values and makes assumptions about initial and final states. The final assertion checks whether the index of new object contains the value x or not. 

In addition, using PexObserve.ValueForViewing, we are sending the pertinent values to the test explorer so that it can visualize them during the test exploration session.

Also, the final assertion checks whether the new object's count remains the same or not - this confirms that neither the count of the elements in the array list has increased nor decreased.

The final assertion `Assert.AreEqual(false, Old_arrListCount > New_arrListCount, "The count of the array should not have changed.");` is a direct check to ensure that the number of elements in the array does not increase or decrease.

Lastly, to run this test, you need to insert this test class and method to your project and run the Pex process. It will generate a set of tests based on the assumptions and constraints you have made. Check the output for any possible combinations and execute the test, the test explorer will show any errors for a failing test or a passing test.
