```

This test checks all return values, counts, indexOf values, and checks if a true return value corresponds with at least one index (since the ArrayList class contracts are not strict about what they return).

#### 4.2.4. Exception Contract Test

Exception contracts allow you to check that the contract for exceptions is respected. For example, consider this code:

```csharp
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOf_ThrowsArgumentNullException([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, Object x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);
            
            [PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentNullException))]
            arrList.IndexOf(x);
        }
    }
}
```

This test checks whether an `ArgumentNullException` is being thrown when you call `IndexOf` on an ArrayList with null as an argument.

#### 4.2.5. Out-Parameters Contract Test

Out-Parameters contract tests are used to check that the contract for out parameters is respected. The method under test will usually only be able to satisfy these contracts if it uses an appropriate method of the class under test, rather than merely returning an appropriate value. For a method that only returns a value, the out-parameters contract is irrelevant.

#### 4.2.6. Structural Contract Test

Structural contracts allow you to assert that certain properties of the class under test have not changed, given a certain input. In Pex, these are quite powerful because they can allow you to check things like whether the list has been re-sorted or resized.

### 4.3. Concepts

Test method,

`PUT_Contains_Invariants`

Assumption,

The assumption is the ArrayList `arrList` contains at least one element `x`.

Observation,

The `arrList.Count` is the number of items in `arrList` before and after the `Contains(x)` call.

Assertion,

The assertion checks whether the `arrList.Contains(x)` method call returns `True` (which it ought to do given the assumption).

Expected Exception,

`IndexOf` method should throw an `ArgumentNullException` when a null is passed as parameter.

### 4.4. Running the Pex Tests

To run the Pex test:

In the Solution Explorer, right-click on the test, choose Run, or press F5. 

This will open the Pex activity monitor which enables you to see the progress tests, debug them if needs, or explore them more in Pex Activity Monitor.

### 4.5. Conclusion

Pex is a powerful tool in performing automated unit and contract tests. It greatly speeds up the testing process as it helps to generate a large amount of test code automatically, while also identifying potential areas for code improvement and bug removal. The flexibility of Pex allows it to be adapted to a variety of testing paradigms, and offers a wide array of features for testing and debugging code.

